I have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Overview
This is an OfficeFloor-based PetClinic REST app. Owner creation is a pipeline of functions wired in `owners.POST.yml`, executed in order: normalizeAddress → require → normalize → normalizeEmail → rejectDuplicate → rejectDuplicateHousehold → rejectCityAtCapacity → **rejectOverDailyLimit** → **build** → assignCustomerCode → **assignMembershipNumber** → countNamesakes → save → respond.

## 1. Files involved in creating/registering an owner where registrationDate is set/defaulted

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` — the pipeline wiring (lines 39-56 wire the daily-limit, build/default-date, and membership-number steps).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java` — maps the DTO to an `Owner` and defaults `registrationDate`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignMembershipNumber.java` — builds membership number using the registration year.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectOverDailyLimit.java` — daily create-limit counting owners by registrationDate.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/DailyLimitExceededException.java` — exception for the limit.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.DailyLimitExceededException.yml` — routes the exception to its handler.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` — responds HTTP 429.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java` — the `registrationDate` / `membershipNumber` fields and accessors (lines 54-55, 60-61, 101-107).
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java` — produces `customerCode`, which the membership number is built on top of (runs immediately before AssignMembershipNumber).

## 2. Defaulting the registration date to the server date

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java`, lines 13-19:
```java
public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, Out<Owner> built) {
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(request);
    if (owner.getRegistrationDate() == null) {
        owner.setRegistrationDate(LocalDate.now());
    }
    built.set(owner);
}
```
When the request has no registration date, it is set to `LocalDate.now()` (server date).

## 3. Membership number generation using the registration year

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignMembershipNumber.java`, lines 13-16:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner) {
    int yy = owner.getRegistrationDate().getYear() % 100;
    owner.setMembershipNumber(String.format("%s-M%02d", owner.getCustomerCode(), yy));
}
```
Format is `<customerCode>-M<YY>` (e.g. `LON-SMI-0007-M26`), where YY is the last two digits of the registration-date year.

## 4. Daily create-limit counting owners per day

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RejectOverDailyLimit.java`, lines 14-31:
```java
public class RejectOverDailyLimit {

    static final int DAILY_LIMIT = 100;

    public void service(OwnerRepository ownerRepository) throws DailyLimitExceededException {
        LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
        int count = 0;
        for (Owner existing : ownerRepository.findAll()) {
            if (today.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate())) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        if (count >= DAILY_LIMIT) {
            throw new DailyLimitExceededException(DAILY_LIMIT);
        }
    }
}
```
It counts all existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today; at 100 or more it throws `DailyLimitExceededException`, which the handler (`DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java`, lines 16-20) turns into HTTP 429. This runs before `build`, so the count is of existing/prior owners only.

## 5. Existing tests for registration date / membership number / create-limit

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java`, lines 14-19 — `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay()`: creates an owner and asserts the returned `registrationDate` is never Saturday/Sunday.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java`, lines 181-187 — `createMany(int n)` helper described as driving "the per-day create-limit / bulk-signup rules"; lines 170-179 `fillCity` and 193-201 owner-fetch helpers support these tests.

I found no dedicated test asserting the membership-number format itself.

## Notable discrepancy
The acceptance test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` (AcceptanceTest.java lines 9-18) and its Javadoc require that the defaulted registration date fall on a business day — "a weekend rolls forward to Monday" and "the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday." However, `BuildOwner.service` (line 16) defaults with a plain `LocalDate.now()` and performs no weekend roll-forward. As written, this test would fail whenever the server date lands on a Saturday or Sunday. No business-day/weekend-rollforward logic exists anywhere in the owner functions.

